1. Scope and definitions

This licence applies to the original code and documentation offered under it,
including covered portions retained in copies, modifications and derived works
(the "Software"). Each copyright holder grants only rights they control.
Separately identified third-party materials remain under their own licences.

"Internal Use" means using the Software privately for your own activities or an
organisation's internal operations, including through employees and contractors
acting on its behalf. It includes use in profitable businesses, internal
administration, productivity improvements, cost savings and support for ordinary
business activities. Those benefits and profits from unrelated goods or services
do not by themselves constitute External Monetisation.

"External Monetisation" means receiving, seeking or arranging revenue or other
valuable consideration in return for an external product or service that contains,
provides access to, or delivers the functionality of the Software. It includes:

(a) selling, renting or licensing copies or products containing the Software,
    including bundled products and modified or renamed versions;
(b) charging for access, subscriptions, hosted services, APIs or paid features
    that use the Software to provide their functionality;
(c) charging external clients for analysis, reports or other services in which
    the Software's functionality or outputs are the offering being supplied; and
(d) advertising, sponsorship, affiliate revenue or other funding received in
    exchange for promotion or benefits associated with that product or service,
    even when users receive it free of charge.

Merely paying an employee or contractor to develop, maintain or operate a private
internal deployment on your behalf does not make your Internal Use External
Monetisation. Using the Software for ordinary internal administration of a
business is distinct from offering its functionality or outputs as a paid service
to that business's clients. An organisation's nonprofit status does not exempt
an externally monetised offering from these conditions.

